ponse code (`response`, stored as text, e.g. \"200\", \"404\", \"503\"), bytes sent (`bytes`), the visitor's IP address (`clientip`), the page requested (`url`, `request`), file type (`extension`), source and destination country (`geo.src`, `geo.dest`), the visitor's browser (`agent`), operating system (`machine.os`) and the server host (`host`).\n\n**2. Dashboard \"EFF-436c0758 Web logs overview\"** – 7 panels (http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-436c0758-web-logs-overview-1f86e55f157c):\n- Single-number panels: total requests, unique visitors (counted by IP address), total bytes served\n- Requests over time, split by response code (bar chart)\n- Share of each response code (pie chart)\n- Top 10 requested URLs, with request count and average bytes (table)\n- Requests by source country (bar chart)\n\n**3. Alert rule \"EFF-436c0758 5xx spike\"** (id `ceb6888e-76e3-45ab-ae7e-442afbda063f`):\n- It runs every minute against `kibana_sample_data_logs`, using `timestamp` as the time field.\n- It looks for requests whose response code starts with \"5\", i.e. any 5xx code.\n- It fires when more than 10 of them arrive within 5 minutes.\n- **Not tested:** this Kibana blocks queries sent straight to the underlying data, so I couldn't confirm that the \"starts with 5\" match finds the 5xx requests. It relies on the sample data's codes being plain text like \"503\".\n- **No notification set up:** the rule has no connector or action, so when it fires the alert only appears in Kibana. Add a connector (Slack, email, etc.) if you want to be told.","ttft_ms":1892,"type":"result","duration_ms":39722,"uuid":"3f663d95-a26e-4012-83db-526c399b1179","ttft_stream_ms":1626,"time_to_request_ms":83,"time_to_request_phases_ms":{"other":21,"system_prompt":8,"resume_other":0,"process_user_input":3,"input_attachments":9,"input_hooks":4,"tool_pool_refresh":2,"init_message":2,"engine_pickup":1,"message_prep":1,"model_config_wait":1,"autocompact":2,"query_setup":0,"model_gates":1,"tool_sch